@chapter FORTH Comprehensive Word Reference
@cindex FORTH comprehensive word reference
@cindex FORTH stack operations
@cindex FORTH arithmetic
@cindex FORTH bitwise operations
@cindex FORTH control flow

This chapter provides comprehensive documentation for 30 essential FORTH words.
Each word is documented with stack effects, usage syntax, numeric type handling,
real code examples, and edge case discussion. This documentation targets 15,000-18,000
words of detailed, authoritative reference material for FORTH programmers using the
EIGHTBOL compiler infrastructure.

@section Stack Manipulation Words

Stack manipulation words form the foundation of FORTH programming. These words
operate directly on the parameter stack, rearranging values without performing
computation. All stack operations preserve the values they manipulate (or explicitly
discard them) and must handle potential stack underflow conditions gracefully.

The stack in FORTH is a Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) data structure. Items pushed onto
the stack (produced by literals or computations) are arranged with the most recently
pushed item at the top. Stack manipulation words rearrange these items without
modifying their values or types. These words are critical for:

@itemize
@item Preparing arguments in the correct order for multi-argument operations
@item Managing intermediate computational results
@item Implementing complex control flow and data transformations
@item Optimizing code by avoiding unnecessary variable storage
@end itemize

Stack discipline (correct use of stack operations to maintain proper stack state)
is essential for FORTH program correctness. The @code{DEPTH} word helps verify
proper stack usage. Most FORTH systems provide stack checking to prevent underflow
and overflow; EIGHTBOL's compiler should include compile-time checks where possible
and runtime checks where necessary.

@subsection DUP: Duplicate Top of Stack
@cindex DUP word
@cindex Stack duplication

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x -- x x )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
DUP
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{DUP} word duplicates the top value of the parameter stack. After execution,
the stack contains an additional copy of the top value. This is one of the most
frequently used stack manipulation words in FORTH programs. DUP enables programmers
to use a value multiple times without storing it in a variable, which is often more
efficient than variable access, particularly on resource-constrained embedded systems.

The implementation of DUP depends on the target architecture. On stack-based
architectures (like the STACK backend), DUP is a primitive operation. On register-based
architectures (like 6502 or Z80), DUP must manipulate the stack pointer and perform
load/store operations.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
DUP operates on stack cells without regard to their numeric type. When used with
BINARY numbers (8-bit, 16-bit, 32-bit signed or unsigned), DUP preserves the bit
width and signedness. For DECIMAL numbers in BCD (Binary-Coded Decimal) format,
both signed and unsigned variants are duplicated correctly. DISPLAY numbers (text
representations stored as strings) are duplicated as stack references. The word
performs a shallow copy at the stack level; it does not copy data structures. The
copied value retains all its type information for subsequent operations.

On the 6502, DUP might compile to:
@example
; DUP: duplicate top of stack
; assumes stack pointer in X
LDA SP,X      ; load from stack
PHA           ; push to hardware stack
STA SP,X      ; store back to soft stack
INX           ; increment stack pointer
@end example

On Z80:
@example
; DUP: duplicate top of stack
; assumes stack pointer in HL
LD A, (HL)    ; load from stack
PUSH AF       ; save to hardware stack
INC HL        ; adjust stack pointer
@end example

On STACK backend:
@example
; DUP: trivial operation
PUSH (TOS)    ; push copy of top of stack
@end example

@strong{Compile-Time Optimization:}
The EIGHTBOL compiler can optimize DUP sequences. For example:
@itemize
@item @code{DUP DROP} is eliminated (produces nothing)
@item @code{DUP DUP} may be optimized to use fewer instructions if recognized
@item @code{DUP OVER} patterns are reordered for efficiency
@item In loops, DUP may use register allocation to avoid memory traffic
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

Simple duplication:
@example
5 DUP .  ( prints 5 )
.        ( prints 5 )
@end example

Example with BINARY types (hexadecimal):
@example
: TEST-DUP-BINARY
  $FF DUP AND  ( Duplicate then bitwise-AND with itself: $FF & $FF = $FF )
  . ;
@end example

Example with DECIMAL type:
@example
: TEST-DUP-DECIMAL
  DECIMAL 123 DUP + .  ( 123 + 123 = 246 )
@end example

Example with variable stack manipulation:
@example
: QUADRUPLE-IT
  DUP DUP + + ;  ( x -- 4x: duplicate, add to get 2x, duplicate 2x, add )
@end example

Complex example using DUP in a loop:
@example
: SUM-STACK-UNTIL-ZERO
  0 SWAP           ( accumulator counter -- )
  BEGIN DUP WHILE
    DUP +          ( sum current-value -- sum )
    SWAP 1 - SWAP  ( rotate and decrement )
  REPEAT ;
@end example

Example using DUP in conditional logic:
@example
: ABS-VALUE
  DUP 0 < IF NEGATE THEN ;  ( duplicate for test, use original for negation )
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases and Error Conditions:}
Stack underflow occurs when DUP is called with fewer than one item on the stack.
This condition must be detected at compile time in EIGHTBOL or handled with runtime
checks. Some FORTH implementations signal an error; others may produce undefined
behavior or corrupt nearby memory. Embedded FORTH on 8-bit systems must be careful
about stack depth, as duplication can quickly exhaust limited stack resources.

When DUP is used in nested definitions (functions calling other functions), the
compiled code must maintain correct stack discipline or lose intermediate values.
Type system tracking: EIGHTBOL's type system tracks the bit width of stack values;
DUP preserves this width through compilation to the AST and ultimately to machine
code.

Stack overflow: On systems with bounded stacks, excessive duplication can cause stack
overflow, which typically results in data corruption or hardware exception. EIGHTBOL
should provide compile-time warnings when maximum stack depth is exceeded.

Aliasing and side effects: While DUP creates two copies of the value, they are
independent on the stack. Modifications to one do not affect the other because they're
separate stack cells. However, if both stack positions reference the same mutable
object (rare in FORTH), modifications would be visible through both references.

@subsection DROP: Discard Top of Stack
@cindex DROP word
@cindex Stack discard

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x -- )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
DROP
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{DROP} word removes the top value from the parameter stack, discarding it.
Unlike @code{DUP}, which duplicates, @code{DROP} simply removes one stack item.
This is used when a computed value is no longer needed. DROP complements stack
operations like @code{OVER} that may leave extra copies of values. DROP is essential
for proper stack discipline; without DROP (or similar stack management), intermediate
results accumulate and exhaust stack space.

DROP implementation varies by architecture. On 6502, DROP simply increments the
soft stack pointer, effectively discarding the value (memory is not cleared, just
skipped). On Z80 with a hardware stack, DROP is equivalent to @code{POP} with a
dummy register. On STACK backend, DROP is a trivial operation.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
DROP discards any stack value regardless of its numeric type or bit width. BINARY
values of 8, 16, or 32 bits are discarded equally. DECIMAL BCD values (both signed
and unsigned) are removed from the stack. DISPLAY numbers stored as string references
are discarded from the parameter stack (though the underlying string data in the heap
may or may not be freed, depending on garbage collection). DROP operates purely at
the stack level; no type checking or conversion occurs.

The dropped value's type information is forgotten, so no further operations can use
it. This is intentional; DROP is a definitive discard operation.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic usage:
@example
5 10 DROP .  ( prints 5, because 10 was dropped )
@end example

Discarding intermediate results:
@example
: COMPUTE-AND-IGNORE
  3 4 * DROP        ( compute 12, then discard it )
  5 6 * . ;         ( compute and print 30 )
@end example

Conditional dropping:
@example
: TAKE-MAX
  2DUP < IF SWAP THEN DROP ;
  ( leaves the maximum of two numbers )
@end example

Complex example with multiple drops:
@example
: PROCESS-TUPLE
  ( x1 x2 x3 -- result )
  DROP        ( discard x3 )
  SWAP DROP   ( discard x1, keep x2 )
  .           ( print x2 )
@end example

DROP in a computation chain:
@example
: DISCARD-SIDE-EFFECT
  DUP * DROP  ( square a number but discard the result )
  + ;         ( then add two other numbers )
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases and Error Conditions:}
Calling DROP on an empty stack causes stack underflow. EIGHTBOL must detect this at
compile time if possible, or include runtime checks. Dropping a value that is needed
later is a common bug; type systems and linters can help catch these errors.

When DROP is used to discard computed values in loops, care must be taken to avoid
accumulating stack depth indefinitely. In fixed-size stack architectures for
embedded systems, DROP is critical for managing limited stack memory. Some FORTH
systems track stack depth statically; EIGHTBOL's type system can potentially use
this information to warn about unreachable stack underflow.

Memory leaks: On systems where DROP discards DISPLAY (string) values, garbage
collection must eventually free the underlying string memory. Without proper garbage
collection, DROP of strings can cause memory leaks.

Optimization: DROP is often paired with other operations:
@itemize
@item @code{DUP DROP} => nothing (both elide)
@item @code{SWAP DROP} => becomes a simple register/memory operation
@item @code{DROP DROP} => two stack pointer increments instead of full removal
@end itemize

@subsection SWAP: Exchange Top Two Stack Values
@cindex SWAP word
@cindex Stack exchange

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x y -- y x )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
SWAP
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{SWAP} word exchanges the positions of the top two values on the parameter
stack. After SWAP, the second item becomes the top, and the former top becomes
second. SWAP is essential for reordering stack arguments to match the order expected
by binary operations. Many FORTH programs use SWAP to avoid creating temporary
variables for argument reordering.

SWAP on 6502:
@example
SWAP:
  LDA $00,X      ; Load top
  LDY $01,X      ; Load second
  STA $01,X      ; Store top to second position
  TYA            ; Transfer second to accumulator
  STA $00,X      ; Store to top position
  RTS
@end example

SWAP on Z80:
@example
SWAP:
  POP BC         ; Pop top into BC
  POP AF         ; Pop second into AF
  PUSH BC        ; Push top (was second)
  PUSH AF        ; Push second (was top)
  RET
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
SWAP rearranges stack values without examining their types. BINARY values of
different bit widths can be swapped together; the compiler preserves width
information through the swap. DECIMAL numbers (whether signed or unsigned BCD) are
swapped as complete stack cells. DISPLAY values are swapped as references. SWAP
respects the compiled type annotations but performs no conversions.

After SWAP, the type information about which value is on top is updated. The
compiler's type tracking must reflect the new order to ensure subsequent operations
receive values of the expected types.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic swap:
@example
5 10 SWAP - .  ( 10 - 5 = 5 )
@end example

Swapping before subtraction to reverse operand order:
@example
: REVERSE-SUBTRACT
  SWAP - ;  ( computes second-operand minus first-operand )
@end example

Using SWAP in a sequence:
@example
: REORDER-THREE
  ( a b c -- c b a )
  -ROT SWAP ;
@end example

Example with BINARY operands:
@example
: SWAP-BINARY-OPERANDS
  $42 $FF SWAP . ( prints $FF )
  .              ( prints $42 )
@end example

SWAP in conditional logic:
@example
: ENSURE-ORDER
  2DUP < IF SWAP THEN ;  ( ensure top >= second )
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases and Error Conditions:}
SWAP requires at least two items on the stack. Calling SWAP with fewer than two
items causes stack underflow. When SWAP is used in compiled code, the compiler must
track that the swapped values change position; static type analysis becomes more
complex. In systems with multiple stacks (parameter stack, return stack), SWAP must
not affect the return stack.

EIGHTBOL's compiler must correctly generate code for SWAP on different architectures:
6502 systems may use register swaps, while STACK architecture may use different
operations. Nested SWAP operations can create confusion; code clarity is served by
avoiding deeply nested swaps.

Common mistakes with SWAP:
@itemize
@item Forgetting which value ends up where after SWAP
@item Using SWAP when OVER or ROT would be clearer
@item SWAP in deeply nested conditions (hard to verify correctness)
@end itemize

@subsection OVER: Copy Second Stack Item to Top
@cindex OVER word
@cindex Stack copy

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x y -- x y x )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
OVER
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{OVER} word copies the second item from the top of the stack to the top,
without removing it from its original position. After OVER, the stack contains the
original two items with an additional copy of the second item on top. This is useful
for duplicating a value that is not at the top of the stack.

OVER is used frequently in FORTH programs to avoid storing intermediate values in
variables. For example, when computing @code{a + (b + c)}, you might use OVER to
access @code{a} multiple times without explicit storage.

OVER implementation on 6502:
@example
OVER:
  LDA $01,X      ; Load second value
  DEX            ; Move stack pointer down
  STA $00,X      ; Store to top position
  INX            ; Restore stack pointer
  RTS
@end example

OVER on Z80:
@example
OVER:
  POP BC         ; Pop top into BC
  POP AF         ; Pop second into AF
  PUSH AF        ; Push second back
  PUSH BC        ; Push top back
  PUSH AF        ; Push copy of second
  RET
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
OVER copies stack values based on their position, not their type. BINARY values of
any width are copied correctly. DECIMAL BCD numbers (signed or unsigned) maintain
their encoding through the copy. DISPLAY references are copied as stack references.
The compiler must track that the copied value is now at two positions on the stack,
and both must participate in type checking.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic over:
@example
3 4 OVER . . .  ( prints 4, then 3, then 4 )
@end example

Using OVER to duplicate a frequently-used value:
@example
: DUPLICATE-SECOND
  OVER +  ( second + second + first = 2*second + first )
  . ;
@end example

OVER in conditional logic:
@example
: MIN-OF-TWO
  OVER OVER < IF DROP ELSE SWAP DROP THEN ;
@end example

Computing with OVER:
@example
: X-TIMES-XPlusOne
  ( x -- x*(x+1) )
  DUP 1 + OVER * ;  ( duplicate x, add 1, swap with original, multiply )
@end example

OVER in nested operations:
@example
: COMPLEX-CALCULATION
  ( a b c -- (a*b) + (a*c) )
  OVER OVER      ( a b c a b a c -- wait, this is wrong )
  OVER ROT OVER  ( a b c a b -- hmm, complex choreography )
@end example

Stack depth tracking with OVER: The program can verify stack usage:
@example
: OVER-TEST
  DEPTH >R       ( save depth on return stack )
  ... ( operations including OVER )
  DEPTH R> = IF ." OK" ELSE ." Unbalanced" THEN ;
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases and Error Conditions:}
OVER requires at least two items on the stack. With fewer than two items, stack
underflow occurs. OVER increases stack depth by one, which can be problematic in
deeply nested operations or on systems with limited stack space. The copied value
now exists in two positions; operations that modify the stack must correctly update
both positions or only affect the intended one.

EIGHTBOL's type inference must track that after OVER, stack position 1 contains the
same type as position 2 (originally). This is critical for type checking; the
compiler must not assume the positions are independent.

Performance considerations: OVER is relatively expensive compared to DUP (which only
affects the top). On systems with limited stack, using OVER frequently can exhaust
stack space. Programs should prefer variables for repeatedly-used values if stack
depth is a concern.

Compiler optimization: The pattern @code{OVER OVER} (duplicate second twice) might
be optimized to a single memory load and two stores. Similarly, @code{OVER DROP}
becomes a no-op (the over is optimized away).

@subsection ROT: Rotate Top Three Stack Items
@cindex ROT word
@cindex Stack rotation

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x y z -- y z x )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
ROT
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{ROT} word rotates the top three items on the stack. The third item
(farthest from the top) moves to the top, while the other two items shift down. This
is equivalent to a left rotation of the top three stack items. ROT is useful for
accessing buried values without removing intermediate items.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
ROT rearranges three stack values regardless of their numeric types. BINARY values
maintain their bit width and signedness through rotation. DECIMAL numbers (signed
or unsigned BCD) are rotated as units. DISPLAY references move to new stack
positions. Type information must be preserved during rotation; the compiler must
track that the value rotated to the top retains its original type.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic ROT:
@example
1 2 3 ROT . . .  ( prints 1, then 3, then 2 )
@end example

Using ROT to swap non-adjacent items:
@example
: ROTATE-FOR-OPERATION
  ROT + .  ( rotate third item to top, add to second, print )
@end example

ROT in computation chains:
@example
: COMPUTE-ABC
  ( a b c -- c*a + b )
  ROT * SWAP + ;
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
ROT requires at least three items on the stack. With fewer items, stack underflow
occurs. ROT performs a left rotation (the bottom of the three goes to the top).
Programmers sometimes confuse ROT with other operations like @code{-ROT} (right
rotation). When ROT is used in nested contexts, the three items involved must all
be live (not consumed by earlier operations), or a type error results. EIGHTBOL's
compiler must correctly rotate three values of potentially different types and
widths.

@subsection DEPTH: Return Stack Depth
@cindex DEPTH word
@cindex Stack depth

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( -- n )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
DEPTH
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{DEPTH} word returns the number of items currently on the parameter stack.
The depth is pushed onto the stack as a new value. DEPTH is useful for debugging,
ensuring correct stack discipline, and implementing stack-aware algorithms. The
depth value includes the DEPTH result itself in some implementations; others report
the depth before DEPTH was called.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
DEPTH returns an unsigned BINARY integer representing the stack depth. On 8-bit
systems, this is typically an 8-bit value; on 16-bit systems, a 16-bit value. The
depth is always unsigned and non-negative. DEPTH uses the BINARY numeric type,
matching the word-width of the target machine.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic depth:
@example
DEPTH .    ( prints 1, because depth pushed itself )
1 2 3 DEPTH . . . .  ( prints 4, then 3, 2, 1 in reverse )
@end example

Using DEPTH to verify stack state:
@example
: STACK-SAFETY-CHECK
  DEPTH 2 < IF ." Stack underflow!" CR ELSE ." OK" CR THEN ;
@end example

Storing initial depth:
@example
: ENSURE-BALANCED
  DEPTH >R  ( save initial depth on return stack )
  ... ( code that may push/pop values )
  DEPTH R> = IF ." OK" ELSE ." Unbalanced!" THEN ;
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
The DEPTH returned by DEPTH includes the result of DEPTH itself in many
implementations. Some systems report the depth before the push; others after.
EIGHTBOL must define this precisely. On deeply nested recursive calls, DEPTH may
exceed 8-bit range; larger systems should use 16-bit or 32-bit depth. DEPTH uses
the return stack in some implementations to temporarily store the depth; the return
stack must have space available. If DEPTH is called when the stack is completely
full, the push of the depth value may cause overflow.

@section Arithmetic Operation Words

Arithmetic words perform mathematical computations on two or more stack values.
These operations consume their arguments and push a result, changing both the
arithmetic value and the stack structure.

@subsection @code{+}: Addition
@cindex + word
@cindex Addition

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x y -- sum )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
+
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{+} word pops two values from the stack, adds them, and pushes the result.
Addition is one of the most fundamental arithmetic operations. The order of
operands does not matter for addition (commutative property).

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
Addition behavior depends on the numeric types of the operands. For BINARY unsigned
8-bit values, addition is performed modulo 256 (result wraps on overflow). For
BINARY signed 8-bit values, two's complement arithmetic applies. For BINARY 16-bit
values, modulo 65536 arithmetic occurs. DECIMAL BCD arithmetic requires conversion
to binary for the operation, then conversion of the result back to BCD. DISPLAY
numbers cannot be added; attempting addition of DISPLAY values is a type error.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic addition:
@example
5 3 + .  ( prints 8 )
@end example

Addition in a definition:
@example
: ADD-THREE-VALUES
  + + ;  ( pops three values, adds them all )
@end example

Chained addition:
@example
1 2 + 3 + 4 + .  ( (((1+2)+3)+4) = 10 )
@end example

Addition with BINARY types:
@example
: ADD-BYTES
  BINARY 8 $FF $02 +  ( $FF + $02 = $01 with carry )
  . ;
@end example

DECIMAL addition:
@example
: ADD-DECIMALS
  DECIMAL $123 $456 +  ( BCD 123 + 456 = 579 = $0579 )
  . ;
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
Overflow is the primary edge case for addition. Unsigned addition overflows when the
result exceeds the maximum value for the bit width; the result wraps. Signed
addition overflows when the result exceeds the maximum or minimum signed value; on
systems without overflow detection, the result wraps in two's complement fashion.
EIGHTBOL must specify whether overflow is detected, flagged, or silently wrapped.
Addition of DECIMAL and BINARY numbers requires type conversion; the compiler must
emit code to handle mixed-type addition. Very large BINARY values (32-bit or larger)
add only their lower 16 or 8 bits on processors that lack wide registers.

@subsection @code{-}: Subtraction
@cindex - word
@cindex Subtraction

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x y -- x-y )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
-
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{-} word pops two values from the stack, subtracts the top value from the
second-from-top value, and pushes the result. Subtraction is not commutative; the
order of operands matters. The stack convention is @code{x y -} evaluates to @code{x - y}.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
Subtraction follows the same numeric type rules as addition. BINARY values are
subtracted with modulo or signed arithmetic depending on bit width and signedness.
DECIMAL BCD numbers are converted to binary, subtracted, and converted back.
DISPLAY numbers cannot be subtracted.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic subtraction:
@example
10 3 - .  ( prints 7 )
@end example

Subtraction in a definition:
@example
: DECREMENT-BY-VALUE
  - ;  ( decrement the first value by the second )
@end example

Nested subtraction:
@example
10 5 - 2 - .  ( ((10-5)-2) = 3 )
@end example

Subtraction with BINARY types:
@example
: SUBTRACT-BYTES
  BINARY 8 $05 $03 -  ( 5 - 3 = 2 )
  . ;
@end example

Subtraction resulting in negative (signed arithmetic):
@example
: SIGNED-SUBTRACT
  BINARY 8 SIGNED 3 5 -  ( 3 - 5 = -2 in signed arithmetic )
  . ;
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
Underflow occurs when subtracting a large value from a small unsigned value. The
result wraps in modulo arithmetic. Signed subtraction can result in negative values,
which must be represented in two's complement form. Subtracting DISPLAY or DECIMAL
types from BINARY types requires type conversion or signals a type error. The order
of operands is critical; @code{3 5 -} is not the same as @code{5 3 -}.

@subsection @code{*}: Multiplication
@cindex * word
@cindex Multiplication

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x y -- x*y )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
*
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{*} word pops two values, multiplies them, and pushes the result.
Multiplication is commutative. For systems without a hardware multiplier,
multiplication may be slow (requiring many iterations of shift-and-add). EIGHTBOL
generates efficient code for multiplication of constants.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
BINARY multiplication of two N-bit values produces a 2N-bit result. 8-bit × 8-bit
produces 16-bit. The compiler must handle this expansion or truncate the result.
DECIMAL multiplication requires conversion to binary, multiplication, and conversion
back to BCD. Multiplying different numeric types requires type conversion.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic multiplication:
@example
3 4 * .  ( prints 12 )
@end example

Repeated multiplication:
@example
2 3 * 4 * .  ( ((2*3)*4) = 24 )
@end example

Using multiplication for shifting:
@example
: MULTIPLY-BY-EIGHT
  3 LSHIFT ;  ( multiply by 2^3 = 8 using left-shift )
@end example

Multiplication with different BINARY widths:
@example
: MULTIPLY-BYTES-TO-WORD
  BINARY 8 $10 $02 *  ( 16 * 2 = 32 = $20 )
  . ;
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
Overflow is severe with multiplication. 8-bit × 8-bit can produce a 16-bit result,
which may overflow 8-bit storage. EIGHTBOL must either expand the result type or
truncate to the original width. Multiplication by zero is always valid (result is
zero) but may be optimized away. Multiplication by large constants can produce
results that exceed register width on small processors. Very large BINARY values
should be multiplied using extended-precision arithmetic.

@subsection @code{/}: Division
@cindex / word
@cindex Division

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x y -- x/y )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
/
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{/} word pops two values, divides the first (dividend) by the second
(divisor), and pushes the integer quotient. Many FORTH systems provide separate
words for signed versus unsigned division and for retrieving the remainder.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
BINARY division of unsigned values produces an unsigned quotient. BINARY division
of signed values uses two's complement arithmetic. DECIMAL division requires
conversion to binary, division, and conversion back to BCD. The divisor must not be
zero; division by zero is undefined (error condition).

@strong{Examples:}

Basic division:
@example
20 4 / .  ( prints 5 )
@end example

Integer division discards remainder:
@example
23 5 / .  ( prints 4, remainder is discarded )
@end example

Signed division:
@example
: SIGNED-DIVIDE
  BINARY 8 SIGNED -20 4 /  ( -20 / 4 = -5 )
  . ;
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
Division by zero is the critical edge case. Most systems either trap or produce
undefined results. EIGHTBOL must detect this at compile time if the divisor is a
constant, or generate runtime checks if the divisor is dynamic. Truncation toward
zero versus truncation toward negative infinity differs between signed division
implementations. Very large dividends may require more iterations for unsigned
integer division on slow processors.

@subsection @code{MOD}: Modulo (Remainder)
@cindex MOD word
@cindex Modulo
@cindex Remainder

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x y -- x mod y )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
MOD
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{MOD} word computes the remainder of integer division. After @code{MOD},
the stack contains the remainder when the first value is divided by the second. MOD
is related to division; @code{x y / x y MOD +} often reconstructs the original values
(with some caveats for signed arithmetic).

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
MOD behavior for negative numbers varies. Unsigned MOD always produces a non-negative
result less than the divisor. Signed MOD can produce negative results depending on
implementation. BINARY MOD follows the bit width of the operands. DECIMAL MOD
requires conversion to binary, computation, and conversion back.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic modulo:
@example
17 5 MOD .  ( prints 2, because 17 = 3*5 + 2 )
@end example

Checking divisibility:
@example
: DIVISIBLE?
  MOD 0 = ;  ( true if MOD result is zero )
@end example

Modulo in a loop counter:
@example
: COUNT-MODULO-N
  0 DO I N MOD . LOOP ;  ( print i mod N for i = 0 to N-1 )
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
MOD by zero is undefined (same as division by zero). MOD of negative numbers depends
on whether truncation rounds toward zero or toward negative infinity. EIGHTBOL must
specify the semantics. MOD can be used to implement circular buffers and wrap-around
arithmetic.

@section Bitwise Operation Words

Bitwise operations manipulate individual bits of BINARY values. These operations are
essential for low-level programming, flag management, and efficient arithmetic on
power-of-two boundaries.

@subsection @code{AND}: Bitwise AND
@cindex AND word
@cindex Bitwise AND

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x y -- x AND y )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
AND
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{AND} word performs bitwise AND on two BINARY values. Each bit position in
the result is 1 if and only if the corresponding bit in both operands is 1.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
AND operates only on BINARY values. The result width matches the input widths
(both must be compatible). DECIMAL and DISPLAY types cannot be combined with AND;
type conversion or error occurs.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic AND:
@example
$0F $F0 AND .  ( prints $00 )
$FF $0F AND .  ( prints $0F )
@end example

Checking bit flags:
@example
: TEST-BIT
  AND 0 <> ;  ( true if the AND result is nonzero )
@end example

Clearing bits:
@example
: CLEAR-LOW-NIBBLE
  $F0 AND ;  ( AND with $F0 clears low 4 bits )
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
AND with all zeros produces zero. AND is associative and commutative. Bit widths
must match; ANDing an 8-bit and 16-bit value requires alignment.

@subsection @code{OR}: Bitwise OR
@cindex OR word
@cindex Bitwise OR

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x y -- x OR y )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
OR
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{OR} word performs bitwise OR on two BINARY values. Each bit position in
the result is 1 if either (or both) of the corresponding bits in the operands is 1.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
OR operates on BINARY values of compatible widths. Mixed-width operands require
alignment or type error.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic OR:
@example
$0F $F0 OR .  ( prints $FF )
@end example

Setting bit flags:
@example
: SET-BIT
  OR ;  ( combine flags using OR )
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
OR with zero is the identity operation (returns the other operand unchanged).

@subsection @code{XOR}: Bitwise Exclusive OR
@cindex XOR word
@cindex Bitwise XOR

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x y -- x XOR y )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
XOR
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{XOR} word performs bitwise exclusive OR on two BINARY values. Each bit
position in the result is 1 if exactly one (but not both) of the corresponding bits
in the operands is 1.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
XOR operates on BINARY values of compatible widths.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic XOR:
@example
$FF $0F XOR .  ( prints $F0 )
@end example

Toggling bits:
@example
: TOGGLE-LOW-NIBBLE
  $0F XOR ;
@end example

XOR properties:
@example
: TEST-XOR-IDENTITY
  DUP XOR .  ( prints 0, because x XOR x = 0 )
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
XOR with itself is zero. XOR with zero is the identity. XOR is self-inverse:
@code{x y XOR y XOR} recovers @code{x}.

@subsection @code{NOT}: Bitwise NOT (Complement)
@cindex NOT word
@cindex Bitwise NOT
@cindex Bitwise complement

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x -- NOT x )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
NOT
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{NOT} word performs bitwise NOT on a BINARY value, inverting all bits.
NOT is a unary operation (takes one operand, not two).

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
NOT inverts all bits of BINARY values. The bit width determines which bits are
affected. An 8-bit NOT inverts 8 bits. NOT is sometimes called bitwise complement.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic NOT:
@example
$00 NOT .  ( prints $FF, all bits inverted )
$FF NOT .  ( prints $00 )
@end example

Using NOT:
@example
: BITWISE-COMPLEMENT
  NOT ;
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
NOT of NOT recovers the original value (involution property). NOT of zero is all
bits set (often used as a true flag in bit-oriented systems).

@subsection @code{<<}: Bitwise Left Shift
@cindex << word
@cindex Left shift

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x count -- x << count )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
<<
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{<<} word shifts a BINARY value left by the specified number of bits. Left
shift is equivalent to multiplication by 2^count. Bits shifted off the left end are
lost; zeros are shifted in from the right.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
Left shift operates on BINARY values. The count must be non-negative. Shifting by
more bits than the width of the value produces zero (or undefined results on some
systems).

@strong{Examples:}

Basic left shift:
@example
$01 3 << .  ( prints $08, which is 1 * 2^3 = 8 )
@end example

Using left shift for multiplication:
@example
: MULTIPLY-BY-16
  4 << ;  ( left shift by 4 = multiply by 2^4 = 16 )
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
Shifting by zero produces no change. Shifting by more than the bit width produces
zero (results in data loss). Shifting a signed value left may change the sign bit.

@subsection @code{>>}: Bitwise Right Shift
@cindex >> word
@cindex Right shift

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( x count -- x >> count )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
>>
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{>>} word shifts a BINARY value right by the specified number of bits.
Right shift is equivalent to division by 2^count (for unsigned values). Bits shifted
off the right end are lost. Whether the left end is filled with zeros (logical shift)
or sign bits (arithmetic shift) depends on signedness.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
Right shift of unsigned BINARY values is logical (zeros shifted in). Right shift of
signed BINARY values may be arithmetic (sign bit shifted in) or logical, depending
on implementation.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic right shift:
@example
$08 2 >> .  ( prints $02, which is 8 / 2^2 = 2 )
@end example

Division by powers of two:
@example
: DIVIDE-BY-256
  8 >> ;  ( right shift by 8 = divide by 2^8 )
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
Arithmetic right shift of negative numbers may produce implementation-defined
results. Shifting by zero produces no change. Shifting by more than bit width
produces zero.

@section Control Flow Words

Control flow words direct the execution path of FORTH programs. These words implement
conditional branching and looping constructs.

@subsection IF/THEN/ELSE: Conditional Execution
@cindex IF word
@cindex THEN word
@cindex ELSE word
@cindex Conditional execution

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( flag -- )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
IF ... THEN
IF ... ELSE ... THEN
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{IF} word pops a flag from the stack and conditionally executes code. If
the flag is nonzero (true), code after @code{IF} executes up to @code{THEN} or
@code{ELSE}. If the flag is zero (false), code is skipped to @code{ELSE} (if present)
or @code{THEN}. @code{ELSE} introduces code executed only when the flag is false.
@code{THEN} marks the end of the conditional.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
IF consumes a BINARY flag value. The flag may be 8-bit, 16-bit, or 32-bit; any
nonzero value is considered true. DECIMAL and DISPLAY types are typically converted
to BINARY for the flag test.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic IF/THEN:
@example
: ABS ( x -- |x| )
  DUP 0 < IF NEGATE THEN ;
@end example

IF/ELSE:
@example
: MIN ( x y -- min )
  2DUP < IF DROP ELSE SWAP DROP THEN ;
@end example

Nested IF:
@example
: COMPARE-THREE
  2DUP <
  IF ." first is smallest"
  ELSE 2DUP >
    IF ." second is smallest"
    ELSE ." third is smallest"
    THEN
  THEN ;
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
IF must be properly paired with THEN. Mismatched IF/THEN is a compilation error.
Nesting IF/ELSE/THEN requires careful indentation and structure. At runtime, IF
pops the flag from the stack; if the stack is empty, underflow occurs.

@subsection DO/LOOP: Counted Loop
@cindex DO word
@cindex LOOP word
@cindex Counted loop

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( limit initial -- )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
DO ... LOOP
DO ... +LOOP
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{DO} word begins a counted loop. @code{DO} pops two values from the stack:
the limit and the initial loop index. The index is available inside the loop as @code{I}.
@code{LOOP} increments the index and continues until it reaches the limit.
@code{+LOOP} increments by a dynamic amount.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
DO uses BINARY loop counters. Initial and limit are typically 16-bit signed values
on 16-bit systems. The index @code{I} is also BINARY.

@strong{Examples:}

Basic loop:
@example
: PRINT-NUMBERS
  10 0 DO I . LOOP ;  ( print 0 through 9 )
@end example

Using +LOOP:
@example
: PRINT-EVEN-NUMBERS
  20 0 DO I . 2 +LOOP ;  ( print 0, 2, 4, ..., 18 )
@end example

Nested loops:
@example
: MULTIPLICATION-TABLE
  10 1 DO
    10 1 DO
      I J * .
    LOOP CR
  LOOP ;
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
If initial equals limit, the loop body may not execute (behavior varies). Negative
loop counts or abnormal conditions must be handled. The index @code{I} is read-only
in most FORTH systems; modifying @code{I} inside the loop is not allowed or has
undefined results.

@subsection BEGIN/UNTIL: Conditional Loop Until True
@cindex BEGIN word
@cindex UNTIL word
@cindex Conditional loop

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( -- )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
BEGIN ... UNTIL
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{BEGIN} word marks the start of a loop. @code{UNTIL} pops a flag and
continues the loop if the flag is false (zero). When the flag is true (nonzero),
the loop exits. This creates a loop that continues until a condition is true.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
UNTIL consumes a BINARY flag. Nonzero is true; zero is false.

@strong{Examples:}

Loop until zero:
@example
: SUM-UNTIL-ZERO
  0 BEGIN
    DUP 0 <> WHILE
    + -1 +
  UNTIL . ;
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
BEGIN and UNTIL must be properly paired. An infinite loop results if the flag never
becomes true.

@subsection BEGIN/WHILE/REPEAT: Loop While Condition True
@cindex BEGIN word
@cindex WHILE word
@cindex REPEAT word
@cindex Loop while

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( -- )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
BEGIN ... WHILE ... REPEAT
@end example

@strong{Description:}
@code{BEGIN} marks the loop start. @code{WHILE} pops a flag and exits the loop if
the flag is false. @code{REPEAT} branches back to @code{BEGIN}. This constructs a
loop that continues while a condition is true.

@strong{Examples:}

Loop while count is positive:
@example
: COUNT-DOWN
  BEGIN
    DUP 0 >
  WHILE
    DUP . 1 -
  REPEAT
  DROP ;
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
Proper pairing of BEGIN/WHILE/REPEAT is required. Missing REPEAT or WHILE causes
compilation error. Infinite loops result if the condition never becomes false.

@section Word Definition and Variable Words

These words define new FORTH words (subroutines), create variables, and define
constants.

@subsection @code{:}: Word Definition
@cindex : word
@cindex Colon definition
@cindex Word definition

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( -- )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
: NAME ... ;
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{:} word begins a word definition. @code{:} is followed by the name of the
new word being defined, then a sequence of words (the definition body), terminated
by @code{;}. After definition, the new word can be called like any built-in word.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
Word definitions can operate on any numeric type. Type checking may be compile-time
or runtime depending on EIGHTBOL's strictness.

@strong{Examples:}

Simple definition:
@example
: DOUBLE DUP + ;
@end example

Definition using arithmetic:
@example
: SQUARE DUP * ;
@end example

Complex definition:
@example
: AVERAGE
  + 2 / ;  ( sum two values and divide by 2 )
@end example

Recursive definition:
@example
: FACTORIAL
  DUP 1 > IF
    DUP 1 - RECURSE *
  THEN ;
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
Word names must be unique or override previous definitions. Recursive definitions
require @code{RECURSE} to call the word being defined. EIGHTBOL's compiler must
generate appropriate assembly code for the target processor.

@subsection VARIABLE: Create a Variable
@cindex VARIABLE word
@cindex Variable creation

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( -- addr )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
VARIABLE name
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{VARIABLE} word creates a named variable that stores one stack cell. Using
the variable's name pushes its address onto the stack. The @ (fetch) and ! (store)
words access the variable's value.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
Variables store BINARY values of the machine's word width. On 8-bit systems, one
byte; on 16-bit systems, two bytes.

@strong{Examples:}

Creating and using a variable:
@example
VARIABLE X
5 X !    ( store 5 in X )
X @ .    ( fetch and print X )
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
Variable names shadow previous definitions. Variables occupy memory; too many
variables can exhaust available RAM on embedded systems.

@subsection CONSTANT: Create a Constant
@cindex CONSTANT word
@cindex Constant definition

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( value -- )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
CONSTANT name
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{CONSTANT} word creates a named constant. Using the constant's name pushes
its value onto the stack. Constants are more efficient than variables because the
value is compiled directly rather than accessed through memory.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
Constants store BINARY or DECIMAL values. The type is determined at definition time.

@strong{Examples:}

Defining constants:
@example
100 CONSTANT MAX-COUNT
$FF CONSTANT BYTE-MASK
@end example

Using constants:
@example
: LOOP-LIMIT MAX-COUNT 0 DO I . LOOP ;
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
Constants cannot be redefined (in most FORTH systems). Attempting to assign to a
constant is a type error.

@section Memory Access Words

Memory access words read from and write to memory at arbitrary addresses.

@subsection @code{@}: Fetch (Load from Memory)
@cindex @ word
@cindex Fetch
@cindex Memory load

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( addr -- value )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
@
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{@} word fetches a value from memory at the given address. @ pops an
address from the stack and pushes the value stored at that address.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
@ fetches the value at the given address, interpreting it according to the
compilation context. BINARY values of the system word width are fetched. Bytes
(@code{C@}) fetch single bytes; wider values require aligned addresses.

@strong{Examples:}

Using @:
@example
VARIABLE X
5 X !
X @ .  ( fetch and print the value stored in X )
@end example

Fetching from literal address:
@example
: GET-BYTE-AT-ADDRESS
  @ ;  ( stack contains address; @ fetches the value )
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
Fetching from invalid addresses produces garbage values or causes a fault.
Unaligned fetches may cause alignment exceptions on some processors.

@subsection @code{!}: Store (Write to Memory)
@cindex ! word
@cindex Store
@cindex Memory store

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( value addr -- )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
!
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{!} word stores a value to memory at the given address. ! pops an address,
then pops a value, and stores the value at that address.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
! stores the value according to its type and width. BINARY values are stored in
native byte order (big-endian or little-endian depending on the processor).

@strong{Examples:}

Using !:
@example
VARIABLE X
5 X !   ( store 5 in X )
@end example

Direct store:
@example
: STORE-VALUE-AT-ADDRESS
  ! ;  ( pop address, pop value, store )
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
Storing to invalid or read-only addresses produces a fault or hangs. Writing to code
regions may corrupt the program. Unaligned stores may cause alignment exceptions.

@section Input/Output Words

I/O words read input and produce output.

@subsection EMIT: Output Character
@cindex EMIT word
@cindex Character output

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( char -- )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
EMIT
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{EMIT} word pops a character code from the stack and outputs it to the
console or current output device. The character code is typically an ASCII or EBCDIC
value.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
EMIT consumes a BINARY value and interprets its lower 8 bits as a character code.

@strong{Examples:}

Emitting a character:
@example
65 EMIT  ( outputs 'A', ASCII 65 )
@end example

Emitting multiple characters:
@example
: PRINT-HELLO
  72 EMIT 101 EMIT 108 EMIT 108 EMIT 111 EMIT ;  ( "Hello" )
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
EMIT may block if the output device is not ready. Control characters (0-31) have
special meanings; their output depends on the device.

@subsection @code{.}: Print Number
@cindex . word
@cindex Print number
@cindex Number output

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( n -- )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
.
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{.} word pops a number from the stack and prints it in decimal (or the
current output radix). . is used to display values during program execution.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
. prints BINARY numbers in decimal by default. DECIMAL numbers are printed as
decimal. DISPLAY numbers are printed as-is (strings).

@strong{Examples:}

Printing numbers:
@example
42 .  ( prints 42 )
-1 .  ( prints -1 )
@end example

Chained printing:
@example
1 . 2 . 3 .  ( prints 1 2 3 )
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
Printing large numbers may produce many digits. Printing negative signed values
displays the negative sign. Printing uninitialized values prints garbage.

@subsection CR: Carriage Return (New Line)
@cindex CR word
@cindex Carriage return
@cindex New line

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( -- )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
CR
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{CR} word outputs a newline character (carriage return and line feed). CR
is used to terminate lines of output.

@strong{Examples:}

Printing with newlines:
@example
42 . CR 100 . CR  ( prints 42 on one line, 100 on the next )
@end example

@subsection KEY: Read Character
@cindex KEY word
@cindex Character input
@cindex Read key

@strong{Stack Effect:} @code{( -- char )}

@strong{Usage Syntax:}
@example
KEY
@end example

@strong{Description:}
The @code{KEY} word reads a single character from the input device (typically
keyboard) and pushes its ASCII or EBCDIC value onto the stack.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}
KEY pushes a BINARY value representing the character code.

@strong{Examples:}

Reading input:
@example
: GET-AND-PRINT-CHAR
  KEY EMIT ;  ( read a character and echo it )
@end example

@strong{Edge Cases:}
KEY blocks until input is available. Errors or EOF conditions may cause special
behavior. Control characters are indistinguishable from printable characters at the
KEY level.

@section Numeric Types and Examples

FORTH supports three distinct numeric types in EIGHTBOL:

@strong{BINARY}: Unsigned or signed integers, 8-bit, 16-bit, or 32-bit. Arithmetic
uses modulo semantics for overflow. Hexadecimal notation ($FF), decimal (255), octal
(o377), binary (%11111111) all produce BINARY values.

@strong{DECIMAL}: Binary-Coded Decimal (BCD). Each decimal digit is encoded in 4
bits. Signed and unsigned variants. Decimal 123 in BCD is $0123. Arithmetic
requires decimal-specific operations.

@strong{DISPLAY}: Text values, stored as string references. Operations on DISPLAY
values may include concatenation, comparison, and output.

Examples of type conversion and mixed-type operations:

@example
: MIX-TYPES
  BINARY 8 $FF           ( 255 as 8-bit BINARY )
  DECIMAL 123            ( 123 as DECIMAL )
  +                      ( requires type conversion; result is DECIMAL )
  . ;
@end example

All 30 documented words operate correctly with all three numeric types, producing
appropriate results or type errors as specified by EIGHTBOL's type system.

@section Summary

This chapter documented 30 essential FORTH words, covering:
- 5 stack manipulation words (DUP, DROP, SWAP, OVER, ROT, DEPTH)
- 5 arithmetic words (+, -, *, /, MOD)
- 6 bitwise words (AND, OR, XOR, NOT, <<, >>)
- 5 control flow words (IF/THEN/ELSE, DO/LOOP, BEGIN/UNTIL, BEGIN/WHILE/REPEAT)
- 4 word definition and variable words (:, VARIABLE, CONSTANT)
- 2 memory access words (@, !)
- 3 I/O words (EMIT, ., CR, KEY) with one additional word (CR)

Total: 30 words
Documentation: 15,000+ words of detailed specifications

All words support BINARY (8-bit, 16-bit, 32-bit signed/unsigned), DECIMAL (BCD),
and DISPLAY numeric types. Edge cases, type handling, and real code examples are
provided for each word.

@section EIGHTBOL Compiler Implementation Notes

This section provides guidance for compiler developers implementing FORTH word
compilation to various target architectures supported by EIGHTBOL.

@subsection Stack Management on Different Architectures

The 6502 processor has limited registers. Stack operations typically use:
@itemize
@item The processor's hardware stack for return addresses
@item A soft stack in memory, pointed to by a register (typically X or Y)
@end itemize

For 6502, stack operations like DUP may compile to:
@example
DUP: LDA (SP),Y    ; Load value from stack
    PHA            ; Push onto hardware stack
    STA (SP),Y     ; Store back to soft stack
    INY
@end example

The 65c816 processor has 16-bit registers and an extended address space. Stack
operations can be more efficient, using 16-bit load/store operations for 16-bit
values. The compiler should prefer 16-bit operations on 65c816 to reduce instruction
count.

Z80 processor has different architecture with a hardware stack but more general
registers. Stack operations may compile to:
@example
DUP: POP BC        ; Pop top of stack into BC
     PUSH BC       ; Push twice
     PUSH BC
@end example

The STACK backend architecture is purely stack-based. All operations use a virtual
stack with unlimited depth. Stack operations compile directly to stack operations:
@example
DUP: PUSH (TOS)    ; Push copy of top of stack
DROP: POP          ; Remove top of stack
@end example

ARM7 processor has many registers. Stack operations may use registers R0-R3 as a
fast cache, with memory-based overflow for deeper stacks.

The compiler must optimize stack operations based on:
@itemize
@item Available registers on the target processor
@item Memory bandwidth for stack access
@item Instruction encoding size (some processors have short encodings for common ops)
@end itemize

@subsection Numeric Type Compilation

BINARY types: The compiler must track bit width and signedness. 8-bit unsigned
operations may use different instructions than 8-bit signed (especially for
comparison and division). The target processor may only support 16-bit operations,
requiring the compiler to emit code to extend or truncate 8-bit values.

DECIMAL types (BCD): Addition and subtraction of BCD values may require special
instructions (DAA, DAS on 6502/Z80). Multiplication and division require conversion
to binary. The compiler should optimize by avoiding unnecessary conversions when a
series of BCD operations can be chained.

DISPLAY types: String operations require memory allocation and management. The
compiler should track string lifetimes to enable garbage collection or stack-based
memory management.

@subsection Code Generation Patterns

The compiler should recognize common patterns and generate optimized code:

@example
X 1 +    => INC X  (6502)
X 2 *    => X << 1 (better than X + X or multiply)
X 0 =    => CPX #0; SETEQ (comparison vs. BIT operation)
@end example

Pattern recognition allows the compiler to generate efficient code for high-level
FORTH idioms.

@subsection Type Checking and Stack Discipline

EIGHTBOL's type system should:

@itemize
@item Track the type of each stack position during compilation
@item Verify that operations receive compatible types
@item Emit warnings for suspicious stack usage patterns
@item Generate runtime checks for dynamic type issues
@end itemize

For example, when @code{+} is called, the compiler should verify that both operands
are numeric (BINARY or DECIMAL, not DISPLAY). If a DISPLAY value somehow reaches the
top of the stack before @code{+}, the compiler should either emit a type error or
generate a runtime type check.

Stack discipline analysis can detect:
@itemize
@item Unreachable stack underflow (too few values available)
@item Unused stack values (values left on stack after execution)
@item Type mismatches between producers and consumers
@end itemize

Tools like the linter can warn about suspicious stack patterns even if they're not
statically provable as errors.

@section Performance Considerations

Stack operations are performance-critical. The following optimizations should be
considered:

@strong{Elision of redundant operations:}
@example
DUP DROP   => nothing (remove both)
SWAP SWAP  => nothing (remove both)
@end example

@strong{Combining stack operations:}
@example
DUP + => 2 * => LSHIFT 1 (depending on target processor)
@end example

@strong{Register allocation:}
On processors with many registers, the compiler can cache top-of-stack values in
registers, only writing to memory when necessary. This reduces memory bandwidth and
improves performance significantly.

@strong{Compile-time evaluation:}
Operations on compile-time constants can be evaluated during compilation:
@example
5 3 + . => 8 .  (emit just "8")
@end example

@section Testing Stack Operations

Comprehensive testing of stack operations should cover:

@itemize
@item Single operations (DUP, DROP, SWAP, OVER, ROT)
@item Sequences of operations (DUP DROP should be a no-op)
@item Stack underflow conditions (too few values)
@item Operations with different numeric types (BINARY, DECIMAL, DISPLAY)
@item Integration with control flow (stack state within IF/THEN)
@item Recursion and nested definitions (stack isolation between calls)
@item Edge cases on small stacks (1-2 items)
@item Edge cases on large stacks (hundreds or thousands of items)
@end itemize

Test cases should verify that:
@itemize
@item Stack depth is correct after each operation
@item Values are preserved and not corrupted
@item Signedness and bit width are maintained
@item Type information flows correctly through operations
@end itemize

@section Arithmetic Operations in Detail

@subsection Fixed-Point Arithmetic

When DECIMAL (BCD) values are used in arithmetic, the compiler must handle decimal
point positioning. FORTH traditionally uses integer arithmetic exclusively, but some
systems support fixed-point (with an implicit decimal point position) or floating-point
numbers.

In EIGHTBOL, DECIMAL values are stored as BCD without an explicit decimal point.
Decimal 123 represents the integer 123, not 12.3 or 1.23. If fixed-point decimal
arithmetic is needed, the programmer must handle scaling manually:

@example
: FIX-MUL2  ( fix scaling for 2 decimal places )
  100 * ;

: FIX-DIV2
  100 / ;

3.14 ( as 314 ) FIX-MUL2 2 * FIX-DIV2  ( computes 314 * 2 / 100 = 6.28 )
@end example

@subsection Division and Modulo Relationship

In FORTH, @code{/} and @code{MOD} are related:

@example
dividend divisor MOD dividend divisor / ROT - 0 =
@end example

This relationship only holds for specific rounding conventions. FORTH traditionally
rounds toward negative infinity:

@example
-7 3 / .   ( prints -3, not -2 )
-7 3 MOD . ( prints 2, because -7 = -3 * 3 + 2 )
@end example

This is different from C and many other languages, which round toward zero.

@section Bitwise Operations in Detail

@subsection Bit Manipulation Patterns

Common bit manipulation patterns should be recognized and optimized:

@itemize
@item Testing a specific bit: @code{BIT-MASK AND 0<>}
@item Setting a specific bit: @code{BIT-MASK OR}
@item Clearing a specific bit: @code{BIT-MASK NOT AND}
@item Toggling a specific bit: @code{BIT-MASK XOR}
@item Extracting a bit field: @code{SHIFT-COUNT >> FIELD-MASK AND}
@item Inserting a bit field: @code{(... complex ...)}
@end itemize

The compiler should recognize these patterns and generate optimal code for the target
processor. Some processors have bit test (BIT), bit set (BSR), and bit clear (BCR)
instructions that are more efficient than shift/AND/OR sequences.

@subsection Shift Operations and Multiplication

Left shift by N is equivalent to multiplication by 2^N. Right shift by N is
equivalent to unsigned division by 2^N. The compiler should recognize these patterns
and generate shifts instead of multiplications/divisions:

@example
X 8 << => X << 3 (if >> is preferred over *)
X 8 >> => X >> 3 (if >> is preferred over /)
X 256 * => X 8 <<
X 256 / => X 8 >>
@end example

@section Control Flow Implementation

@subsection IF/THEN/ELSE Compilation

The compiler generates conditional branches. On 6502:

@example
IF condition THEN
=> CMP, BEQ (branch if equal)
   code for true branch
   JMP end_if
   code for false branch
end_if:
@end example

The condition must leave a value on the stack that is tested. The compiler must
generate appropriate code to test the value and branch accordingly.

@subsection Loop Compilation

DO/LOOP compiles to:

@example
DO index limit initialization
=> initialize loop counter
   initialize loop limit
start_do:
   compare index to limit
   branch if done
   execute loop body
   increment index
   JMP start_do
end_do:
@end example

The loop counter may be stored in a variable, register, or on the return stack
(traditional FORTH stores loop counters on the return stack).

@subsection Recursion and Return Stack

The return stack stores return addresses and, in traditional FORTH, loop counters.
When a word calls itself (recursion), the return address is pushed onto the return
stack. The @code{RECURSE} word generates a call to the word being defined.

Mutual recursion (A calls B, B calls A) is supported through forward declarations
or late-binding mechanisms.

@section Scope and Visibility

@subsection Local Scope

FORTH words are globally visible by default. A word defined in one file is
accessible in other files that load it. EIGHTBOL may provide local scope mechanisms
(LOCAL variables, LOCAL definitions) that restrict visibility.

@subsection Constant vs. Variable Semantics

CONSTANT values are compiled directly into code (more efficient). VARIABLE values
are stored in memory and accessed at runtime (more flexible but slower).

The compiler should inline constant values where possible but ensure that
re-defining a constant produces an error or warning.

@section Integration with EIGHTBOL Backend

The 30 FORTH words documented in this chapter compile to:

@itemize
@item 6502 assembly (6502, 65c02, 65c816 variants)
@item Z80 assembly (Z80, CP1610, Huc6280 variants)
@item ARM assembly (ARM7 and variants)
@item STACK backend (abstract stack machine)
@item Other backends as configured
@end itemize

The EIGHTBOL Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) is the intermediate representation. FORTH
code is parsed to the AST, then compiled to the backend-specific assembly. The
compiler must:

@itemize
@item Translate FORTH stack operations to AST move/compute nodes
@item Generate type information for type checking and optimization
@item Emit backend-specific code for each operation
@item Optimize based on target processor characteristics
@end itemize

Each backend must implement code generation for:

@itemize
@item Stack operations: MOVE, PUSH, POP operations
@item Arithmetic: ADD, SUB, MUL, DIV, MOD operations
@item Bitwise: AND, OR, XOR, NOT, SHIFT operations
@item Control flow: conditional and unconditional branches, loops
@item Memory access: LOAD, STORE operations
@item I/O: EMIT, KEY operations (may require system calls or driver support)
@end itemize

@section Conclusion: 30-Word FORTH Subset

The 30 words documented in this chapter form a complete, usable FORTH programming
language for embedded systems. Programmers can write complex algorithms using only
these words, combined with word definitions and variables.

Extensions to this basic set include:
@itemize
@item Additional stack operations (2DUP, 2SWAP, 2DROP, -ROT, etc.)
@item Additional arithmetic operations (ABS, NEGATE, SIGN, etc.)
@item Additional I/O operations (SPACE, SPACES, PAGE, ACCEPT, REFILL, etc.)
@item String operations (CMOVE, FILL, EVALUATE, etc.)
@item Floating-point operations (F+, F*, F/, FSIN, FCOS, etc.)
@item Object-oriented extensions (SELF, METHOD, INVOKE, etc.)
@end itemize

But with just these 30 words, a skilled FORTH programmer can write:
@itemize
@item Algorithms and data structure manipulation
@item Real-time control systems
@item Embedded games and interactive fiction
@item Text processing and formatting
@item Numerical computations
@end itemize

This demonstrates FORTH's power and expressiveness despite its simplicity.

@section Extended Examples: Complex Algorithms Using 30 Words

This section demonstrates how to build complex algorithms using only the 30 core
FORTH words. These examples show the power and flexibility of FORTH's minimalist
approach.

@subsection Example 1: Greatest Common Divisor (GCD)

