@subsection Example 2: Fibonacci Sequence

Computing the Nth Fibonacci number:

@example
: FIBONACCI ( n -- fib(n) )
  DUP 1 >
  IF
    DUP 1 - FIBONACCI SWAP
    DUP 2 - FIBONACCI +
  ELSE
    DROP 1
  THEN ;
@end example

This is a naive recursive implementation. It's simple but inefficient for large N.
A better iterative approach:

@example
: FIBONACCI-ITER ( n -- fib(n) )
  DUP 1 > IF
    1 1 SWAP       ( f0=1, f1=1, count=n )
    2 DO
      OVER + SWAP  ( f0+f1, f0 )
    LOOP
    DROP           ( discard f0, keep f1 )
  ELSE
    DROP 1         ( fib(0) = fib(1) = 1 )
  THEN ;
@end example

@subsection Example 3: Sum of Array Elements

Summing an array of numbers (stored in memory):

@example
: SUM-ARRAY ( addr count -- sum )
  0 SWAP           ( sum=0, loop count )
  0 DO
    DUP @          ( sum, current address, fetch value )
    ROT +          ( current address, new-sum )
    SWAP 1 +       ( new-sum, next address )
  LOOP
  DROP ;           ( discard address, leave sum )
@end example

Usage:
@example
: TEST-ARRAY
  10 CONSTANT VALUES
  VALUES !  ( store address of VALUES )
  3 1 + VALUES !  ( VALUES[0] = 3 )
  5 1 + VALUES !  ( VALUES[1] = 5 )
  7 1 + VALUES !  ( VALUES[2] = 7 )
  ( ... etc )
  VALUES @ 3 SUM-ARRAY .  ( print sum of 3 elements )
@end example

@subsection Example 4: Linear Search

Finding an element in an array:

@example
: FIND-ELEMENT ( addr count target -- found-flag )
  0 SWAP           ( found=0, loop count )
  0 DO
    DUP ROT DUP @ = IF
      DROP -1      ( set flag to true )
      LEAVE        ( exit loop - not available in 30-word set! )
    ELSE
      1 +          ( increment index )
    THEN
  LOOP
  DROP DROP ;
@end example

Note: This example shows a limitation: the 30-word set lacks LEAVE to exit loops early.

@subsection Example 5: Simple Sort (Bubble Sort)

Sorting an array using bubble sort:

@example
: BUBBLE-SORT-PASS ( addr count -- )
  1 - 0 DO
    DUP I +  DUP @ ( addr, addr+i, value[i] )
    OVER 1 + @ DUP ROT < IF
      ( swap needed: value[i] > value[i+1] )
      OVER 1 + !    ( store value[i+1] at addr+i )
      SWAP !        ( store value[i] at addr+i+1 )
    ELSE
      DROP DROP     ( no swap, clean stack )
    THEN
  LOOP ;

: BUBBLE-SORT ( addr count -- )
  DUP 0 DO
    2DUP BUBBLE-SORT-PASS
    1 -    ( decrement remaining count )
  LOOP
  DROP DROP ;
@end example

@section Error Handling and Stack Safety

@subsection Compile-Time Stack Checking

The EIGHTBOL compiler can perform compile-time analysis to detect stack underflow,
overflow, and imbalance errors. During parsing and compilation, the compiler tracks
stack depth:

@itemize
@item Constants and literals push a value (+1 stack depth)
@item Stack operations (DUP, SWAP, DROP, OVER, ROT) modify depth by known amounts
@item Arithmetic operations (0x, /, etc.) consume two and produce one (-1)
@item IF/THEN/ELSE branches require specific stack depths at entry and exit
@end itemize

When stack depth goes negative (underflow), the compiler should emit an error at
compile time rather than producing incorrect code.

Example:
@example
: BAD-CODE
  DUP DUP DROP DROP DROP ;  ( four drops but only three duplicates: underflow! )
@end example

The compiler detects that after @code{3 DUP} operations and @code{4 DROP} operations,
the stack depth is negative, and reports an error.

@subsection Runtime Stack Checking

When compile-time analysis is insufficient (e.g., with dynamic branches or loops),
runtime checks can verify stack safety. The generated code can check stack depth
before critical operations:

@example
; Runtime check before DUP:
LDA stack_depth
CMP #$01
BLT stack_underflow
@end example

Runtime checks have a performance cost but provide guaranteed safety for complex
programs.

@subsection Stack Overflow Detection

Stack overflow occurs when the stack grows beyond available memory. Detection requires:

@itemize
@item Monitoring stack pointer against a limit
@item Checking after PUSH/DUP operations
@item Raising an exception or halting if overflow occurs
@end itemize

On embedded systems with limited RAM, stack overflow is a serious concern. The
compiler should estimate maximum stack depth and warn if it approaches available
memory.

@section Optimization Strategies for Stack Code

@subsection Dead Code Elimination

Operations on values that are never used can be eliminated:

@example
5 DUP DROP  =>  5 DROP  (DUP is dead, output never used)
5 DROP       =>  nothing (no side effects)
@end example

@subsection Constant Folding

Operations on constants can be evaluated at compile time:

@example
5 3 + .  =>  8 .  (compile-time addition)
$FF $0F AND .  =>  $0F .  (compile-time bitwise)
@end example

@subsection Common Subexpression Elimination

Repeated subexpressions can be computed once and reused:

@example
: ORIGINAL
  X @ DUP + DUP + .  ( load X, duplicate twice, add, add, print )

: OPTIMIZED
  X @ DUP + DUP + .  ( same code; no opportunity here )

: BETTER-ORIGINAL
  ( 2 * X 2 * X + )  ( 4 * X: could simplify to X 4 * )
@end example

@subsection Register Allocation

On register-based processors, the compiler should allocate frequently-used stack
values to registers, reducing memory traffic:

@example
: LOOP-HEAVY
  100 0 DO
    I I + .          ( loop body: load I, duplicate, add, print )
  LOOP

; With register allocation, I might stay in a register,
; reducing load/store operations.
@end example

@subsection Strength Reduction

Replace expensive operations with cheaper ones:

@example
X 2 * => X << 1   (shift is usually faster than multiply)
X 4 / => X >> 2   (shift is usually faster than divide)
X 1 + => X 1-     (increment/decrement have dedicated instructions on some CPUs)
@end example

@subsection Inlining

Small, frequently-called words can be expanded inline to eliminate call overhead:

@example
: DOUBLE DUP + ;  (2 instructions: good candidate for inlining)

(Before inlining)
: USE-DOUBLE
  5 DOUBLE . ;
  => CALL DOUBLE, then RET, then .

(After inlining)
: USE-DOUBLE
  5 DUP + . ;
  => DUP +, then .
@end example

@section Type System Design for FORTH

@subsection Type Inference

The compiler can infer types from context:

@example
: EXAMPLE
  5 3 +    ( 5 and 3 are literals => BINARY; + needs BINARY => result is BINARY )
  . ;      ( . consumes BINARY )
@end example

@subsection Type Conversions

Mixing types requires explicit conversion or automatic coercion:

@example
: MIX-TYPES
  BINARY 5 DECIMAL 10 +  ( requires conversion )
@end example

The compiler must emit conversion code when mixing BINARY and DECIMAL types.

@subsection Type Checking Modes

Strict: All type mismatches are errors. Permissive: Automatic conversions where
possible. The compiler mode affects code generation and error reporting.

@section Language Extensions Beyond 30 Words

While the 30-word subset is complete, useful extensions include:

@strong{Additional Stack Operations:}
@itemize
@item @code{2DUP}: Duplicate top two items
@item @code{2DROP}: Discard top two items
@item @code{2SWAP}: Swap top two pairs
@item @code{NIP}: Drop second item
@item @code{TUCK}: Copy top below second
@item @code{-ROT}: Right rotation (opposite of ROT)
@end itemize

@strong{Additional Arithmetic:}
@itemize
@item @code{ABS}: Absolute value
@item @code{NEGATE}: Unary minus
@item @code{MAX}: Maximum of two values
@item @code{MIN}: Minimum of two values
@item @code{SIGN}: Sign of value (-1, 0, or 1)
@end itemize

@strong{Additional I/O:}
@itemize
@item @code{SPACE}: Output a space
@item @code{SPACES}: Output multiple spaces
@item @code{ACCEPT}: Read a line of input
@item @code{REFILL}: Refill input buffer
@item @code{."}: Emit a string literal
@end itemize

@strong{String Operations:}
@itemize
@item @code{CMOVE}: Copy memory block
@item @code{FILL}: Fill memory with value
@item @code{EVALUATE}: Evaluate a string as FORTH code
@item @code{PARSE}: Parse a string
@end itemize

@strong{Floating-Point:}
@itemize
@item @code{F+}, @code{F-}, @code{F*}, @code{F/}: Floating-point arithmetic
@item @code{FSQRT}: Square root
@item @code{FSIN}, @code{FCOS}: Trigonometric functions
@end itemize

@strong{Object-Oriented Extensions:}
@itemize
@item @code{:METHOD}: Define a method
@item @code{SELF}: Reference to self
@item @code{INVOKE}: Call a method
@item @code{SUPER}: Call parent method
@end itemize

@section Conclusion on 30-Word Subset

@section 6502 Stack Machine Code Generation

The 6502 processor is a classic 8-bit CPU with limited registers and a 256-byte
zero page (fast memory). The EIGHTBOL compiler targets the 6502 by using a soft
stack in memory. Typically:

@itemize
@item The zero page location $00-$01 holds the soft stack pointer (2 bytes, 16-bit address)
@item A 256-byte region or larger holds the actual stack
@item The hardware stack (at $0100-$01FF) holds return addresses only
@end itemize

The compiler must:

@enumerate
@item Preserve the 6502's limited register set (A, X, Y only)
@item Minimize memory accesses (stack access is slower than register operations)
@item Emit short instructions (6502 instructions vary from 1-3 bytes)
@item Handle signed/unsigned arithmetic correctly (6502 has no built-in signed support)
@end enumerate

For DUP on 6502:
@example
; X = soft stack pointer
DUP:
  LDA $00,X     ; Load top of stack
  STA $01,X     ; Write back to new location
  INX            ; Increment pointer
  RTS
@end example

For + on 6502:
@example
ADD:
  DEX                ; Pop Y from stack
  LDA $00,X
  STA $00          ; Temporary: Y
  DEX                ; Pop X from stack
  CLC                ; Clear carry for addition
  ADC $00          ; Add Y
  STA $00,X         ; Store result
  INX                ; Increment stack pointer
  RTS
@end example

The 6502 addition uses CLC/ADC for unsigned arithmetic. Signed arithmetic requires
additional code to handle overflow flags and sign extension.

@section 65c816 Extended Addressing

The 65c816 extends the 6502 with 16-bit registers and a 24-bit address space. This
allows more efficient code:

@itemize
@item Use 16-bit A register for 16-bit operations
@item Use larger soft stacks beyond the 64K boundary
@item More efficient long addressing
@end itemize

For DUP on 65c816 (16-bit mode):
@example
DUP:
  LDA $0000,X    ; Load 16-bit value from stack
  STA $0002,X    ; Write to new stack location
  INX 2           ; Increment by 2 (16-bit cell)
  RTS
@end example

@section Z80 Stack Management

The Z80 processor has a hardware stack and more general-purpose registers. Stack
operations can use the hardware stack directly or manage a separate data stack.

For DUP on Z80:
@example
DUP:
  PUSH AF        ; Save accumulator
  PUSH HL        ; Save HL (used for addressing)
  LD HL, (SP+2)  ; Load top of stack
  PUSH HL        ; Push copy
  POP HL         ; Restore HL
  POP AF         ; Restore accumulator
  RET
@end example

The Z80 approach differs from 6502 because Z80 has a powerful hardware stack. Some
implementations use the hardware stack directly for FORTH operations, while others
maintain a separate data stack in memory.

@section ARM7 Register-Rich Architecture

ARM7 has 16 general-purpose registers. Stack operations can cache stack values in
registers for performance:

For DUP on ARM7:
@example
; Assume R13 = stack pointer (SP convention)
; R0-R3 = fast cache for top 4 stack values
; Further stack values stored in memory at [SP]

DUP:
  STMFD SP!, {R0}  ; Push R0 (top of stack) to memory
  MOV R1, R0       ; Copy to R1 (now second-from-top)
  RET              ; Return to caller
@end example

The ARM7 approach uses registers as a cache. The top value is in R0, second-from-top
in R1, etc. When the cache is full, additional pushes spill to memory.

@section STACK Backend (Purely Abstract)

The STACK backend is a purely abstract stack machine with unlimited depth. It
requires no register allocation or memory management:

@example
DUP stack-instructions
  PUSH (TOS)  ; Push top of stack

+
  POP x       ; Pop both operands
  POP y
  PUSH (x + y) ; Push result
@end example

The STACK backend is useful for:

@itemize
@item Testing FORTH compilation without hardware constraints
@item Educational purposes
@item Prototyping new features before implementing on real hardware
@item Debugging complex FORTH programs
@end itemize

@section Type System Integration

The EIGHTBOL type system tracks:

@itemize
@item Numeric type (BINARY, DECIMAL, DISPLAY)
@item Bit width (8, 16, 32, or larger)
@item Signedness (signed or unsigned)
@item Stack position (depth tracking)
@end itemize

Type checking can occur at:

@itemize
@item Compile time: Catch type errors before code generation
@item Link time: Verify consistency across modules
@item Runtime: Check types dynamically with a performance penalty
@end itemize

The compiler must emit appropriate type conversions when mixing types. For example,
adding a BINARY and DECIMAL value requires:

@enumerate
@item Convert DECIMAL to BINARY
@item Perform addition
@item Convert result back to DECIMAL (if needed)
@end enumerate

@section Garbage Collection and Memory Management

On systems with limited memory, the FORTH runtime must manage memory efficiently.

Options include:

@itemize
@item Stack-based allocation: Memory is allocated on a stack; deallocation is automatic when a frame ends
@item Reference counting: Each object tracks how many references point to it; when count reaches zero, memory is freed
@item Mark-and-sweep: Periodically scan all reachable objects; free unmarked objects
@end itemize

For embedded systems, stack-based allocation is often preferred due to its simplicity
and predictability.

@section Standard Forth Compliance

The 30 words documented in this chapter are based on the ANS Forth standard, with
some modifications for EIGHTBOL's multi-architecture support and numeric type system.

Key ANS Forth compatibility notes:

@itemize
@item Stack effects are documented using the standard notation: @code{( before -- after )}
@item Word names follow ANS Forth conventions where applicable
@item Numeric behavior follows ANS Forth for most operations
@item Some operations (like DECIMAL type handling) extend ANS Forth
@end itemize

Programmers familiar with ANS Forth should find the EIGHTBOL FORTH dialect familiar,
with a few extensions and clarifications for embedded systems programming.

@section Advanced Techniques

@subsection Tail Call Optimization

When a word ends with a call to another word, the compiler can optimize by jumping
directly to that word rather than calling and then returning:

@example
: A
  ... (computation)
  B ;  ; call B

(without optimization)
=> CALL B
   RET

(with tail call optimization)
=> ... (computation)
   JMP B  ; jump directly, no return
@end example

@subsection Inline Expansion

Short words can be inlined to avoid function call overhead:

@example
: DOUBLE DUP + ;

(call site uses DOUBLE)
=> CALL DOUBLE
   ...

(after inlining)
=> DUP +
   ...
@end example

@subsection Peephole Optimization

The compiler can recognize patterns and replace them with more efficient sequences:

@example
DUP DROP  => (remove both)
X 1 + => INC X (on 6502)
X 2 << => X << 1 + X << 1 (use addition instead of shift if shift is slow)
@end example

@subsection Profile-Guided Optimization

If FORTH programs are profiled to identify hot paths (frequently executed code), the
compiler can generate optimized code for those paths. For example:

@itemize
@item Frequently used words get inline expansion
@item Hot loop bodies get special compilation
@item Rarely-used words are compiled for code size instead of speed
@end itemize

@section Testing and Verification

Comprehensive testing of the 30 FORTH words includes:

@subsection Unit Tests

Each word should have unit tests covering:

@itemize
@item Basic functionality
@item Edge cases (empty stacks, maximum/minimum values)
@item Type handling (BINARY, DECIMAL, DISPLAY)
@item Error conditions
@end itemize

Example unit test for DUP:
@example
TEST: DUP-BASIC
  5 DUP = 2 DEPTH  ( duplicate 5, check both values exist )

TEST: DUP-STACK-UNDERFLOW
  DUP  ( should error with empty stack )

TEST: DUP-NUMERIC-TYPES
  BINARY 8 $FF DUP AND $FF = ( DUP preserves type )
  DECIMAL 123 DUP + 246 = ( DUP works with DECIMAL )
@end example

@subsection Integration Tests

Test combinations of words:

@example
TEST: DUP-DROP-IDENTITY
  5 DUP DROP = ( value unchanged )
  DEPTH 0 = ( stack empty )

TEST: DUP-OVER-SWAP
  1 2 DUP OVER SWAP
  = ( produces 1 2 2 1 on stack )
  ...
@end example

@subsection Regression Tests

As bugs are fixed, add tests to prevent re-introduction of those bugs:

@example
TEST: DUP-REGRESSION-ISSUE-123
  ( specific case that previously failed )
  ...
@end example

@subsection Architecture-Specific Tests

Test the generated code on each target architecture:

@itemize
@item 6502: Verify stack operations don't corrupt zero page
@item Z80: Verify the hardware stack is used correctly
@item ARM7: Verify register allocation doesn't lose values
@item STACK: Verify the abstract machine produces correct results
@end itemize

@section Performance Benchmarks

Performance of FORTH operations varies by architecture. Benchmarks should measure:

@itemize
@item Instruction count (lower is better)
@item Cycle count (lower is better)
@item Memory usage (lower is better)
@item Code size (lower is better)
@end itemize

Example benchmark for DUP:
@example
: BENCH-DUP
  0 SWAP
  1000 0 DO
    DUP  ( 1000 duplications )
  LOOP
  DROP ;
@end example

Benchmark results guide optimization decisions. If DUP is frequently used but slow,
optimizing DUP has high impact.

@section Debugging FORTH Programs

EIGHTBOL should provide debugging support:

@itemize
@item Stack trace: Show the call stack at each step
@item Step execution: Execute one word at a time
@item Stack inspection: Display stack contents
@item Breakpoints: Pause execution at specific words or conditions
@item Single stepping: Execute one machine instruction at a time
@end itemize

These debugging features are essential for developing complex FORTH programs on
embedded systems where printf() debugging is difficult.


@section Performance Analysis: Speed vs. Code Size Trade-offs

When targeting embedded systems, the choice of how to compile FORTH words affects
both speed (cycle count) and code size (memory usage). Each target architecture has
different performance characteristics.

@subsection DUP Performance Characteristics

@strong{6502 (stack in memory via index register):}
Approximate cycles: 6-8, depending on clock speed and memory access time.
Code size: 5-6 bytes

@strong{65c816 (16-bit mode, 16-bit stack cells):}
Approximate cycles: 11 due to multiple memory operations.
Code size: 10-12 bytes

@strong{Z80 (hardware stack):}
Approximate cycles: 32, but no soft stack pointer management needed.
Code size: 6 bytes

@strong{ARM7 (register-based with spilled stack):}
Approximate cycles: 1-2 in best case, when cache is full.
Code size: 4 bytes

@strong{STACK backend (abstract):}
Approximate cycles: 1 abstract cycle
Code size: 1 instruction (platform-independent)

@section Benchmarking Real FORTH Programs

@subsection Benchmark: Sum of Array Elements

Computing the sum of 1000 unsigned 16-bit integers:

Expected performance across architectures:
@itemize
@item 6502: ~40 ms (at 1 MHz clock)
@item 65c816: ~20 ms (at 4 MHz)
@item Z80: ~30 ms (at 4 MHz)
@item ARM7: ~3 ms (at 50 MHz)
@end itemize

@subsection Benchmark: Recursive Fibonacci

Computing fib(20) recursively demonstrates algorithm complexity:

@itemize
@item 6502: ~5 seconds (very slow due to exponential recursion)
@item 65c816: ~2 seconds
@item Z80: ~3 seconds
@item ARM7: ~200 ms
@end itemize

The naive recursive implementation is O(2^n), making it impractical for large N.

@section Language Compliance and Standardization

The 30-word FORTH subset is designed with compatibility in mind:

@strong{ANSI Forth Compliance:} The subset is largely compatible with ANS Forth,
with some extensions for EIGHTBOL's multi-target architecture and numeric type system.

@strong{Forth 2012 Standard:} Future versions of EIGHTBOL might adopt subsets of
the Forth 2012 standard for additional capabilities.

@strong{Deviations from ANS Forth:}
@itemize
@item Numeric type system (BINARY, DECIMAL, DISPLAY) extends ANS Forth
@item Multi-target compilation is unique to EIGHTBOL
@item Some words have extended stack effects to handle type information
@item The STACK backend is specific to EIGHTBOL
@end itemize

@section Recommendations for Programmers

When developing FORTH programs with EIGHTBOL:

@enumerate
@item Verify stack discipline at each step using DEPTH
@item Use profiling to identify performance bottlenecks
@item Prefer iteration to deep recursion on limited-memory systems
@item Test on target hardware, not just the STACK backend
@item Use compile-time constants (CONSTANT) instead of runtime lookups (VARIABLE)
@item Document stack effects for all custom words
@item Use meaningful word names that reflect their purpose
@end enumerate

@section Recommendations for Compiler Developers

When implementing EIGHTBOL FORTH compilation:

@enumerate
@item Perform compile-time stack depth analysis
@item Implement comprehensive error checking
@item Optimize for the target processor (register allocation, instruction selection)
@item Provide debugging support (stack trace, stepping)
@item Benchmark generated code against hand-written assembly
@item Test all 30 words on all target architectures
@item Document deviations from ANS Forth clearly
@end enumerate

This completes the comprehensive documentation of the 30 FORTH words forming
the core of EIGHTBOL's FORTH language support.

